Pants-Type Diapers Witness Strong Adoption
The market is segmented by type into tapes type, pants type, and hybrid diapers.
Pants-type diapers are rapidly gaining popularity across Asia-Pacific, particularly among parents of active toddlers. Pull-up designs provide greater flexibility and convenience as children begin crawling, walking, and becoming more physically active.
Countries including China, Japan, and South Korea have witnessed strong adoption of pants-type products. Leading manufacturers have invested heavily in pants-style product lines across both premium and mid-range categories.
The growing emphasis on mobility, comfort, discreet diapering, and ease of use is expected to support continued demand for pants-type diapers during the forecast period.
Disposable Diapers Continue to Dominate Applications
Based on application, the market is divided into disposable baby diapers, cloth baby diapers, biodegradable diapers, and others.
Disposable baby diapers maintain their leading position due to increasing urbanization, expanding dual-income households, and greater consumer awareness of infant hygiene.
Product innovation is also supporting the segment. Manufacturers are introducing thinner absorbent cores, enhanced leakage protection, improved breathability, and hypoallergenic materials to increase comfort and performance.
At the same time, biodegradable diapers are emerging as an important growth segment as environmental concerns influence consumer purchasing decisions.
Toddlers Represent an Attractive Consumer Segment
The market is segmented by end user into newborns aged 0–6 months, infants aged 6–12 months, and toddlers aged 12+ months.
Toddlers represent a commercially significant segment because of their longer diapering period and relatively high product consumption. As children become increasingly active, parents seek products that offer flexibility, mobility, secure fitting, and leakage protection.
Premium toddler diapers featuring enhanced skin protection and breathable materials are particularly attractive to higher-income households in developed Asian markets.
Super Absorbent Polymer Remains a Key Material
By material, the market includes non-woven fabric, wood pulp, super absorbent polymer (SAP), and bamboo and plant-based fibers.
Super absorbent polymer remains a dominant material because of its ability to retain large amounts of liquid and maintain dryness. SAP technology is particularly important in premium diaper products where absorbency and leakage prevention are major purchasing considerations.
Meanwhile, bamboo and plant-based fibers are creating new opportunities for manufacturers seeking to develop environmentally differentiated products.
